,Dp,Dq,Dr,Ds,Dt,Du,Dv,Dw,Dx,Dy,Dz,DA,DB,DC,DD,DE,DF,DG,DH,DI,DJ,DK,DL,DM,DN,DO,DP,DQ,DR,DS,DT,DU,DV,DW,DX,DY,DZ,D_,D$,Ea,Eb,Ec,Ed,Ee,Ef,Eg,Eh,Ei,Ej){return {layout:pi,data:[{staticPage:"\u003C!DOCTYPE html PUBLIC \"-\u002F\u002FW3C\u002F\u002FDTD XHTML 1.0 Transitional\u002F\u002FEN\" \"http:\u002F\u002Fwww.w3.org\u002FTR\u002Fxhtml1\u002FDTD\u002Fxhtml1-transitional.dtd\"\u003E\n\u003Chtml xmlns=\"http:\u002F\u002Fwww.w3.org\u002F1999\u002Fxhtml\" xml:lang=\"en\" lang=\"en\"\u003E\n    \u003Chead\u003E\u003Clink rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"\u002F600\u002Fcss\u002Fstatic.css\"\u003E\n        \u003Ctitle\u003EAstrophysics Science Division - X-ray Astrophysics Research\u003C\u002Ftitle\u003E\n\n\n\n\n\u003C\u002Fhead\u003E\n    \u003Cbody class=\"astro lnnine-one-on\" id=\"buson\"\u003E\n        \u003Cdiv id=\"bkg_surround\"\u003E\n            \u003Cdiv id=\"bkg_layer\"\u003E\n            \u003C\u002Fdiv\u003E\n        \u003C\u002Fdiv\u003E\n        \u003Cdiv id=\"paperTop\"\u003E\n        \n\n\n\u003Cdiv class=\"clear\"\u003E\u003C\u002Fdiv\u003E\n\n            \n            \n            \u003Cdiv id=\"headimage\"\u003E\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\n\n\t\t\t\u003C\u002Fdiv\u003E\n          \n          \n          \u003Cdiv class=\"clear\"\u003E\u003C\u002Fdiv\u003E\n         \n\n          \n            \u003Cdiv class=\"clear\"\u003E\u003C\u002Fdiv\u003E\n          \u003C\u002Fdiv\u003E\n    \n          \u003Cdiv id=\"paper\"\u003E \n            \u003Cdiv id=\"contentwrapper\"\u003E\n                \u003C!-- Content Wrapper, this div surrounds the main content and the Left Nav --\u003E\n                \u003Cdiv id=\"rightcontent\" class=\"whiteback\"\u003E\n                    \u003Ca name=\"skipping\" id=\"skipping\"\u003E\u003C\u002Fa\u003E\n\n\n\n\n\u003C!-- \n**********************************\n**** START MAIN CONTENT HERE. ****\n**********************************\n--\u003E\t\n\t\t\u003Ch2 class=\"txonly\"\u003EGalaxy Clusters\u003C\u002Fh2\u003E\n\t\t    \n\t\u003Ch2\u003EGalaxy Clusters\u003C\u002Fh2\u003E\n\n\n Galaxy clusters are the most massive gravitationally bound objects in\nthe Universe, with masses of up to a thousand trillion times that of our sun\n(10^15 Msun) and extending millions of light years. Though called clusters\nof galaxies (and indeed containing tens of thousands of galaxies), most of\ntheir mass is actually dark matter. We learned of that in 1930s, when Zwicky\nrealized that galaxies in clusters move so fast that they must be held\ntogether by a much stronger gravity field then their own. Today, we can also\nobserve X-ray emission from the tenious, hot (10-100 million degrees)\nintergalactic plasma that is trapped in the potential well of the cluster.\nIt confirms the dominance of dark matter in clusters and in the Universe as\na whole, since the matter content in clusters is representative of the whole\nUniverse.\n\n\u003C\u002Fp\u003E\n\u003C\u002Fp\u003E\n\nThe intracluster plasma consists mostly of primordial hydrogen and helium,\nbut it also has traces of heavier elements, such as oxygen and iron,\nproduced by stars inside the galaxies. Those elements are driven out from\nthe galaxies into the intracluster medium by supernovae winds and ram\npressure exerted as the galaxies move through the intracluster medium. When\nwe observe an energy spectrum of the X-ray emission from clusters, we see\nemission lines produced by those elements. Those lines contain a wealth of\ninformation about the physics of galaxies and the intracluster plasma. For\nexample, the Doppler shifts and broadening of those lines reveal the flows\nand turbulence in the plasma. The figure shows an X-ray spectrum of the\nPerseus Cluster observed with Hitomi, the first instrument capable of\nobtaining high-resolution spectra for extended objects such as clusters;\nmultiple narrow lines of Iron and Nickel are seen. Our lab is working on the\nHitomi successor, XRISM, and on the future X-ray spectrometers such as\nAthena. We also work on imaging the intracluster plasma in X-rays using\nChandra and XMM-Newton, and modeling their evolution using supercomputers.\n\n\u003Cdiv class=\"grid-item\"\u003E\u003Cimg src=\"662web_per_spec.jpg\" width=\"400\" alt=\"Hitomi Persus Cluster Spectrum\" \u003E\u003C\u002Fdiv\u003E\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\t\u003C!-- \n**** END MAIN CONTENT HERE. ****\n--\u003E\n\n\n                \u003C\u002Fdiv\u003E\n                \u003C!-- END Main content div --\u003E\n          \u003Cdiv id=\"leftcontent\"\u003E\n                    \u003C!-- Left content div (left side, includes the left nav) --\u003E\u003C!-- Begin Left Nav --\u003E\n                    \n                  \t\t  \n                        \u003C!-- END Left Nav --\u003E\n                \u003C\u002Fdiv\u003E\n                \u003C!-- END Left content div --\u003E\n            \u003C\u002Fdiv\u003E\n            \n            \u003C!-- END Content Wrapper, this div surrounds the main content and the Left Nav --\u003E\n\n\n        \u003C\u002Fdiv\u003E\n\n\n    \u003C\u002Fbody\u003E\n\u003C\u002Fhtml\u003E\n",title:"Astrophysics Science Division - X-ray Astrophysics Research",fluid:m,internal:m}],fetch:{},error:a,state:{application:{fluidPages:[pj,"spaceGPS","stafflist","project","projects","scienceNuggets","scienceNuggetDetail"],name:ez,nameshort:ez,slide_image:"\u002Fcontent\u002FuploadFiles\u002Forganization_slide_0\u002F662.avif",menu:m,orgMenu:m,fullMenuContentToggle:m,additionalContentDisplay:m,org:b_,orgLast:b,contacts:[{phone:"301.286.5016",sl_first_name:pk,sl_middle_initial:bm,sl_last_name:"Porter",email_address:"frederick.s.porter@nasa.gov",role_disp:kt},{phone:"301.286.3258",sl_first_name:pl,sl_middle_initial:"Marshon",sl_last_name:"Penny",email_address:"alicia.m.penny@nasa.gov",role_disp:"Project Support Specialist"}],official:{name:"Ryan Joseph Smallcomb",link:"\u002Fastrophysics\u002Fxray\u002Fbio\u002Fryan.j.smallcomb"},curator:{name:"J.D.  Myers",link:"\u002Fastrophysics\u002Fxray\u002Fbio\u002Fjohn.d.myers"},webmaster:{name:"Ben  Pelletier",link:"\u002Fastrophysics\u002Fxray\u002Fbio\u002Fbenjamin.e.pelletier"},ContactText:a,color:pm,offWhiteColor:pn,aColor:po,image:pp,aColors:{"600":"#3D719E","610":"#36725E","660":po,"670":"#956B23","690":"#9B491C"},colors:{"600":"#2d5273","610":"#316855","660":pm,"670":"#a07326","690":"#7f3c18"},colorsOffWhite:{"600":"#E2EBF4","610":"#DAECE6","660":pn,"670":"#F5EAD6","690":"#F8E7DD"},images:{"600":"\u002Ffiles\u002Fapp-img\u002Fbg600.jpg","610":"\u002Ffiles\u002Fapp-img\u002Fbg610.jpg","660":pp,"670":"\u002Ffiles\u002Fapp-img\u002Fbg670.jpg","690":"\u002Ffiles\u002Fapp-img\u002Fbg690.jpg"},downtime:{content:b},customPageText:{"404":b,testing:b,ecss:b,ecssPreview:b,scienceNuggets:b,scienceNuggetsPreview:b,scienceNuggetDetail:b,ecssSpotlight:b,ecssLegacy:b,spaceGPS:b,stafflist:a,stafflistphoto:a,bio:b,management:b,orglist:a,orgchart:a,calendar:a,projects:a,projectsfeatured:a,projectsalpha:a,project:b,pressreleases:a,presentations:a,reports:a,localnews:a,awardsnominate:a,awardswon:a,awards:a,award:b,featuredimages:a,images:a,eduresources:b,apps:b,publications:a,publicationslegacy:a,faq:b,sitemap:b,api:b,contact:b,documents:b,howdoi:a,forms:a,safetysecurity:a,computinginfo:a,techfacilities:a,researchareas:b,researcharea:b,search:b,overview:b,static:b,home:b},orgOverview:"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cb\u003EX-ray Astrophysics Laboratory\u003C\u002Fb\u003E conducts research in observational astrophysics and on X-ray instruments and optics, operates a suite of existing X-ray missions, and performs mission design and instrument\u002Foptics development for future missions.\u003C\u002Fp\u003E\r\n\u003Cp\u003E\r\n\t\u003Ca href=\"\u002F662\u002Fpeople\u002Findex.html\"\u003E\u003Cimg src=\"\u002F662\u002Fgroup2.jpg\" width=\"400\" alt=\"X-ray Astrophysics Lab Group Photo\"\u003E\u003C\u002Fa\u003E\u003Cbr\u003E\r\n\t\u003Ca href=\"\u002F662\u002Fpeople\u002Findex.html\"\u003E\u003Cb\u003EX-ray Astrophysics: Who we are\u003C\u002Fb\u003E\u003C\u002Fa\u0